8|0@WorldofTanks^1440255933#cubu06l:AvatarOfMomus$:Yeah.... Really this kind of expectation is a problem in game development in general. It's assumed that because you work on a game you should be as much of an expert as someone who spends probably 20 or more hours a week playing it, which is massively unrealistic for a number of reasons. Just for a start someone could be an expert on the code of the game but have little practical experience playing it. They could also have a level of understanding about certain mechanics that surpasses what any player could know but at the expense of general knowledge. Even someone in a lead designer roll is going to be too busy looking at the big picture to keep the mechanical minutia of the game in their head. Plus it assumes that someone who spends between 40 and 60 hours a week making a game wants to spend another 20 or more playing it, which is generally not the case and a good way to burn yourself out, or that a game company wants to pay for its employees to play the game for fun, which is also generally not the case and not as useful as one might think.
